00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,640
 In this lesson, I will talk about how to control what parts of your project GitHub Co-Pilot

2
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:08,240
 and see and use.

3
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:09,240
 Let's get started.

4
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:14,080
 We'll start by talking about the dot getting your file.

5
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:21,360
 How it works, white matters, and how it helps you keep your workspace secure and efficient.

6
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:27,520
 GitHub Co-Pilot uses your workspace context to provide better answers, search your code,

7
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:31,280
 and understand how your files connect.

8
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:34,880
 But sometimes you don't want everything to be included.

9
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:42,520
 For example, files that contain secrets or large-built folders that aren't relevant to your code.

10
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:45,120
 That's where the dot getting your file comes in.

11
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:51,160
 It tells both Git and Co-Pilot, which files and folders should be ignored during indexing

12
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:52,800
 and search.

13
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:58,440
 Sales and listed here will be excluded from Co-Pilot's workspace index and won't appear

14
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:01,520
 in workspace searches or code suggestions.

15
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:04,440
 Unless you open those files directly.

16
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:09,040
 Now let's talk about why you might want to ignore files.

17
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:12,520
 There are two main reasons, security and performance.

18
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:19,360
 For security as a dot getting your file helps make sure that sensitive files like API keys,

19
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:25,440
 credentials, or local configuration files, don't get included in Co-Pilot's context

20
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:29,440
 or accidentally shared in code examples.

21
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:35,720
 Also Co-Pilot doesn't upload your private code to external servers for training, excluding

22
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:40,800
 these files still adds an extra layer of safety.

23
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:47,120
 For performance, ignoring unnecessary folders like node modules, dist or coverage keeps

24
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:51,520
 Co-Pilot's workspace index, smaller and faster.

25
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:56,480
 This means quicker responses and more relevant suggestions that focus on your actual source

26
00:01:56,480 --> 00:01:57,480
 code.

27
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:01,760
 Let's go over how to configure your dot getting your file.

28
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:04,360
 Creating or updating it is simple.

29
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:07,480
 It just uses plain text patterns.

30
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:11,600
 You can ignore single files, folders or file types.

31
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:19,320
 For example, adding the line config.json, ignores one specific file named config.json.

32
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:23,800
 It tells heat and Co-Pilot to completely skip that file.

33
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:29,320
 If you add this slash, it ignores the entire dist folder and everything inside it.

34
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:33,280
 You usually do this for build or compiled files.

35
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:41,160
 Adding start.log ignores all files ending with .log, for instance, server.log or error.log won't

36
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:42,560
 be tracked.

37
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:49,360
 Using double start/logs tells git to ignore any folder named logs, no matter where it is.

38
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:54,840
 Whether it's at the root or deep inside another folder, all will be ignored.

39
00:02:54,840 --> 00:03:01,320
 Finally, adding an exclamation mark before a pattern means don't ignore.

40
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:05,880
 So if everything else is ignored, the app folder will still be included.

41
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:10,640
 Now how does Co-Pilot actually use the .kittigner file?

42
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:15,320
 When you run a workspace search or ask a pilot a question about your project, it looks

43
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:19,320
 only at files not ignored by .kittigner.

44
00:03:19,320 --> 00:03:24,480
 Ignored files are skipped during indexing and won't show up in semantic searches.

45
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:30,480
 However, if you open an ignored file manually, Co-Pilot can still use that files content

46
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:35,280
 in your current chat or completion, because it's now active in your editor.

47
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:39,860
 This matches GitHub's official behavior and is designed to help you when you're working

48
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:42,520
 with that file intentionally.

49
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:47,600
 There's one more thing to be aware of, negation limitations.

50
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:52,960
 If you ignore an entire folder you can't re-include files inside it unless you change

51
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:53,960
 the pattern.

52
00:03:53,960 --> 00:04:00,880
 For example, if you ignore the whole public folder and then try to include style.css,

53
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:04,440
 it won't work because the folder itself is already excluded.

54
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:08,200
 So that's how .kittigner works with GitHub Co-Pilot.

55
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:14,800
 A simple but powerful way to keep your AI assistant focused on the right files.

56
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:20,560
 By ignoring sensitive or unnecessary files, you make Co-Pilot faster, safer and much more

57
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:24,440
 efficient at understanding your real code.

58
00:04:24,440 --> 00:04:28,560
 I hope this lesson was useful for you and see you in the next lesson.

